<Site title="Livelihoods and unequal access"><PageHeader title="Who bears the cost?" eyebrow="Livelihoods & access" description="We follow documented effects through farms, work, prices and household services, while keeping plausible pathways separate from measured loss." /><section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="grid-3"><article class="editorial-card"><p class="eyebrow">Farms & food</p><h3>From rain to output</h3><p>A dry period can affect crops differently by place, crop stage and irrigation. Sowing, harvest and price records are needed to identify an actual effect.</p></article><article class="editorial-card"><p class="eyebrow">Work & business</p><h3>Shared supply chains</h3><p>Power, transport, cooling and municipal delivery can create common constraints. We need documented disruptions before calling them current impacts.</p></article><article class="editorial-card"><p class="eyebrow">Households</p><h3>Different buffers</h3><p>Storage, purchased supply, treatment, time and tenure may change what households can do. No representative household cost is assumed here.</p></article></div></div></section><section class="section"><div class="shell grid-2"><div><h2>What money may buffer—and where evidence is still needed</h2><p>Private storage or purchased alternatives can sometimes bridge a gap, but their actual use, cost and quality must be documented locally. The same shared source, pipe network or quality failure may constrain households with very different incomes.</p><p>We have not published a national cost or loss total. Correlated losses across sectors cannot simply be added, and a rain anomaly is not proof of inflation or migration.</p></div><div class="empty-panel"><h3>Reporting needed before a place-specific claim</h3><ul><li>Dated service and price records</li><li>Original local reporting and attributed accounts</li><li>Crop and market series with matched periods</li><li>Counterevidence and recovery signals</li></ul><a href={url('/journalists/')}>See the document request desk ↗</a></div></div></section></Site>
